\date{2026-04-13T21:28:16Z}
\tag{discrete}

\import{discrete-macros}

\taxon{Remark}
\p{It’s worth spelling out how to write this in formal predicate logic: ##{\forall n \in \Z^+ \st \mathrm{Composite}(n) \implies \exists p
    \in \Z^+ \st \mathrm{Prime}(p) \land (p \mid n) \land p \leq \sqrt
    n.} The phrase “it has a prime divisor #{\leq \sqrt n}” actually expands into an #{\exists} with a conjunction of three propositions! Each of “prime”, “divisor”, and “#{\leq \sqrt
  n}” corresponds to a separate property of the number that is claimed to exist.}


